_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "I'll add a regression test for the reported value and the other values `round()` got wrong.\nFixed: `round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`.\n\n**Cause:** `round(x, 2)` worked on the binary float, and `2.675` is actually stored as `2.67499999\u2026`, so it rounded down. `round()` also rounds exact ties to the even digit (`0.125 \u2192 0.12`), which goes against the \"half up\" rule in the function's own description.\n\n**Fix** (`pricing/money.py`): the function now takes the number as it's normally printed (`2.675`, not `2.67499999\u2026`) and rounds it to cents with `Decimal`, always rounding halves up. It still returns a `float`, so `add_vat` and other callers don't need changes. Negative halves round away from zero (`-2.675 \u2192 -2.68`).\n\n**Checks:** I couldn't run pytest because it isn't installed here, so I ran the checks directly in Python instead:\n- `2.675\u21922.68`, `1.005\u21921.01`, `0.125\u21920.13`, `-2.675\u2192-2.68`, `2.674\u21922.67`, `2.665\u21922.67`.\n- `add_vat(10.0)` still gives `12.0`.\n- The existing `test_split` passes.\n- I added `tests/test_round.py` with these cases and checked the same values by hand.\n\n**Limitation:** the fix rounds whatever float it receives. In `add_vat`, `x * (1 + rate)` is float maths, so a product meant to land exactly on a half-cent can come out slightly off and round the other way. Making that exact would mean doing the VAT calculation in `Decimal` too; I left that alone because it's beyond what you asked for.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 10, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 250130, "requests_cache_write": 39598, "requests_cost_usd": 0.30032400000000004, "requests_count": 10, "requests_input_uncached": 22, "requests_output": 2611, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "n